[Chapter "Main line (4.Nc3 dxc4)"]
[Line "Krause Attack: 6.Ne5"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Czech Variation, Carlsbad Variation"]

{White recaptures on c4 with the knight and eyes the e4 square. Black answers with a central break.} 1. d4 d5 {Claim the centre.} 2. c4 {The Queen's Gambit: White attacks d5.} 2... c6 {The Slav: support d5 with a pawn and keep the c8 bishop's diagonal open, unlike ...e6.} 3. Nf3 Nf6 {Develop and guard d5 again.} 4. Nc3 dxc4 {Take on c4. White will win the pawn back, but meanwhile Black gets ...Bf5 in.} 5. a4 {Stops ...b5, which would hold on to the pawn.} 5... Bf5 {The light-squared bishop comes out before ...e6: the key Slav idea.} 6. Ne5 {White wants to win c4 back with the knight and follow up with f3 and e4.} 6... Nbd7 {Challenge the e5 knight straight away.} 7. Nxc4 Qc7 {Control e5 and prepare ...e5 to free Black's game.} 8. g3 e5 {The freeing break: Black strikes in the centre before White can play e4.} *
